re where in 2005 people started  talking about this when some of the  first scandals came up about electronic  voting machines that have been rolled  out about three years prior and then  there's this whole history of  bureaucracy where it takes about seven  years to go from like establishing a  task force to like getting the Election  Commission to agree this is something  that we should do but from there it's  starting to move a little bit a little  bit we got a little bit of money to do a  planning phase and last year a technical  advisory committee was formed which I  sit on that's that's why I'm here that's  how I fit into this where we've got some  some tech people from around the Bay  Area together to advise the city  government and how they should go about  this project because the people that run  elections in San Francisco and OH are  people who know about election is not  necessarily people who know about  software so in 2018 we didn't get as  much money budgeted for this as we want  it we had hopes for four million dollars  we've got 1.7 million dollars over two  years allocated to development of this  project so it's really exciting and so  the the next steps on that are actually  that the city is gonna hire a project  manager but more about that in a bit  this project has been supported by a lot  of organizations and this is a very  hilarious slide to me because it's like  one third of it as tech companies that  people in this room recognized like  github and like you know organizations  like the open source initiative and then  there's like you know the San Francisco  Democratic Party and like the Harvey  Milk LGBT Democratic Club and then  there's other advocacy organ  like a national election defense  coalition which like most people only  know that like one-third of these people  exist in there a different third for  every person so we've got like an  eclectic coalition of people behind this  and we've got some nice press coverage  about it but it's kind of an out of this  slide because I'm trying to find the  date on this this is November 26 2015 so  I should update this this slide it's  kind of an old headline and more but  more recently this summer the civil  grand jury released a report about open  stars voting in San Francisco where they  made some good recommendations and how  we can move this forward so there's some  attention coming to this and as I said  there is now some dollars behind it as  well which is exciting so the the first  iteration that we're proposing here  because you should develop this  iteratively that's how things work in  the real open-source software world is  start with vote-by-mail ballots San  Franciscans love to vote by mail in the  November 2016 election 63% of our votes  were cast by mail and in this past June  it was almost 65 percent and I think  that's just going to keep going up and  we'll hit 2/3 before 2020 so if you're  able to do vote by mail ballots only  you're actually covering like the vast  majority of ballots already it's it is  the predominant use case so to do that  you need surprisingly little you need  hardware and software for a scanner and  in particular need hardware for a  high-volume scanner you need you know  software for layout and coding and  tabulation and results reporting and  some auditing and batch management stuff  and supporting that I'm not going to  talk about but you need basically the  basics to make sure that you can  interpret and count votes but the appeal  of this is that you only need to roll  this out on about four to six devices  instead of like 600 devices because  you're not doing polling places and  you're 46 devices are going to be in the  basement of City Hall operated by a  select group of trained people rather  than like be located in random parking  garages where they're going to be  operated by everyday people and just  literally random voters would walk in so  it's a lot easier to get this kind of  stuff right where you can where you can  train the people you only need to buy a  couple of ice  you don't need to have like hundreds of  devices that you need to track  throughout the city I need to be  completely foolproof so this is gonna be  easier to phase in except despite then  then just like polling place scanners is  especially going to be easier than  accessible devices because accessibility  is hard people have all sorts of issues  disability issues that you never  imagined and it's really hard to get  that kind of stuff right and in addition  to that we want to do modular  procurement as well we want to be able  to like put out different parts of this  system to different vendors and and  integrate it together later we want to  be able to switch vendors halfway  through if we're not happy so by  building the system and pieces you're  also doing that and with this you can  sort of prove that the system works by  spending like you know ten percent of  your money on sixty four percent of your  use case so that's that's a great  trade-off we think so currently we have  our technical advisory committee we have  our monthly meetings and we develop  recommendations but our recommendations  document is actually on github because  we practice what we preach we eat our  own dog food etc so you too can submit a  poor request to our recommendations  document if you think something should  be in there and we welcome that there  was a feasibility study that was  commissioned last year that came back  earlier this year with a super flot  report that we've shot full of holes  that had outrageous cost estimates which  we're now trying to disprove by making  some proofs of concept ourselves  committing convincing government people  to actually spine money as hard  especially if they see scary numbers but  we got 1.7 million dollars in the city  budget as I said for the 2018 2020  budget so we've got something to move  forward with and the city is now  recruiting to hire a project manager for  this project and hopefully that's one of  you there was also some proposals for  getting state matching funds from the  state of California for this project but  that unfortunately had died but  nevertheless the city has put some of  its own money to that so that's that's  been good and you too can help us make  this make us can help us make this a  reality you can support us by signing up  as  which I will have sheets where you can  put down your email address and get our  get our newsletter and everything you  can follow s of Oakland voting on  Twitter for for updates and also because  having more followers makes us look good  because that's the way things work you  can contribute by submitting pull  requests to our recommendations at OS  VTAC that could have that i/o and you  can apply to be the project manager  working for the city of San Francisco  too to help make this project a reality  or if you don't want to do that you can  encourage your friends to do so so a  bitly slash voting job we have that job  description up and I hope that we can  get someone someone good in that  position because that'll be really  important and finally if you're not in  San Francisco you can advocate for this  locally you can build support in your  county or in your state  talk to your elected officials and your  election administrators and try to get  them to see that this is important and  at collaborating on software that we can  all use that is not and not owned by  companies to run our elections is  important so that's all I got my  contacts atop of the slide if you want  to get in touch with me about this and  then as I said apply for the apply for  the project manager job spread the word  about it follow us on Twitter and our  recommendation document is on that URL  and more information is there as well  and you too can submit poor request to  it so thank you very much for listening  and I hope you found this exciting  [Applause]
 

